    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "1655470f_18257553",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/core.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 694,
      "author": {
        "id": 1761165
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-17T22:59:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I want to emulate your code snippit above while confining changes to kernel/sched/core.c.  I think this might be close:\n\nEssentially, housekeeping_any_cpu can possibly return an online but inactive CPU, and your seems to be to change it to not return an inactive cpu if possible.\n\nThis should do the same:\n\n        if (default_cpu \u003d\u003d -1) {\n                default_cpu \u003d housekeeping_any_cpu(HK_FLAG_TIMER);\n                if (!cpu_active(default_cpu)) {\n                        default_cpu \u003d cpumask_any_and(cpu_active_mask);\n                        if (default_cpu \u003e\u003d nr_cpu_ids)\n                                default_cpu \u003d cpumask_any(cpu_online_mask);\n                }\n        }\n\nFirst, we get a housekeeping CPU.  It may bail out of the numa check, and find nothing, in which case it will return an online cpu.  That online cpu might be inactive, so here we\u0027re restarting to take any active cpu.  Which will likely always return a good cpu.  But in the strange event that there is nothing active, we drop back to taking anything that\u0027s online.  And since this processor is running, it should be online.  \n\nTechnically this should probably check the result of cpu_online_mask, since the api allows for the return of no cpu.  I\u0027ll add that check, but it should never happen.\n\nI think that\u0027s basically the idea you had, and it should prevent this from overflowing into isolation.c.\n\nI\u0027ll push this (and the fix I need above) and we can discuss further.",
      "parentUuid": "1f601ed5_b98b96df",
